| Path | Top holder | Share | Lines | Owner line |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `lib/` | Troy Goode (2 addresses) | 66.8% (159) | 238 | yes |
| `test/` | Troy Goode (2 addresses) | 53.5% (517) | 966 | yes |
| `.github/` | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 32.2% (118) | 366 | **nobody holds half** |
| root files | Troy Goode | 32.3% (134) | 415 | **nobody holds half** |

- **`.github/`:** Wilson holds 32.2% and Íñigo Marquínez Prado holds 26.0%. I suggest no owner line.
- **Root files:** Troy holds 32.3% and Wilson holds 27.7%. I suggest no owner line. A root-file CODEOWNERS pattern would also match every directory, so the tool skips it.
- **Bus factor:** This is the smallest number of authors who together hold 50% of surviving lines. It is 1 for `lib/` and 1 for `test/`. It is 2 for the root files and 2 for `.github/`.

**What the figures leave out**
- **Reformat commit:** `62628c0` ("style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes", Fiona Formatter, 291 lines) is ignored. Counting it, Fiona would hold 14.7% of the repo.
- **Bots:** One bot identity is excluded (10 lines, 11 commits).
- **Mechanical rewrites:** I checked for them and set aside only `62628c0`. Five other commits look mechanical and were kept:
  - `739dc11`, Wilson, 17 lines
  - `fcd2b56`, Troy, 16 lines
  - `00d6eec`, Wilson, 9 lines
  - `344ce1e`, Wilson, 1 line
  - `9c2b59d`, Wilson, 1 line
- **Copy detection:** The figures use `git blame -w -M -C`, and the tool reported no copy-detection shifts. All 18 files were counted, with 1,985 of 1,995 lines attributed.

**Identities:** Nothing is written to `.mailmap`, so the merge exists only in memory.
- **Troy Goode:** The same name appears at `<email redacted>` (762 lines, 93 commits) and `troy@laptop.example` (48 lines, 2 commits). The tool rates this as strong evidence.
- **alex94puchades:** This is probably the same person as Alejandro Caravaca Puchades, because they share an address. I did not merge them. In `lib/` they show as `alex94puchades` with 16 lines (6.7%), which doesn't change any owner.

These figures show who wrote the lines that survive. They don't show who should review changes. Please review the owner lines before you commit them. For the owner lines, consider a GitHub handle or team instead of a bare email, because CODEOWNERS matches handles more reliably.

